WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.047
The main lesson is to choose string
libraries by contract and use them consistently.

2
00:00:05.127 --> 00:00:09.611
A safer API is valuable when its behavior
is documented, understood, and followed.

3
00:00:09.691 --> 00:00:13.790
It is not enough to replace an old
function name with a newer one and assume the

4
00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:14.575
work is done.

5
00:00:14.655 --> 00:00:18.369
Track the facts that matter: destination
capacity, content length,

6
00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:21.844
null termination, pointer lifetime,
mutability, ownership,

7
00:00:21.924 --> 00:00:25.639
allocation behavior, truncation behavior,
and failure reporting.

8
00:00:25.719 --> 00:00:27.384
When those facts are explicit,

9
00:00:27.464 --> 00:00:30.474
string code becomes easier to review and
easier to test.

10
00:00:30.554 --> 00:00:32.860
Make edge cases part of normal validation.

11
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.591
Test exact-fit buffers, empty strings,
full buffers, truncation,

12
00:00:36.671 --> 00:00:40.514
embedded nulls where relevant, allocation
failure, malformed input,

13
00:00:40.594 --> 00:00:42.067
and platform differences.

14
00:00:42.147 --> 00:00:46.567
Check return values, and decide what
partial output means before it reaches later

15
00:00:46.647 --> 00:00:47.031
logic.

16
00:00:47.111 --> 00:00:49.353
Finally, document project conventions.

17
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:50.778
Which APIs are approved?

18
00:00:50.858 --> 00:00:52.843
How are wrappers expected to behave?

19
00:00:52.923 --> 00:00:54.460
What happens on truncation?

20
00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.013
Who owns returned buffers?

21
00:00:56.093 --> 00:00:57.823
Which platforms are supported?

22
00:00:57.903 --> 00:01:02.386
Safe string-library usage is a
maintainable practice built from clear contracts,

23
00:01:02.466 --> 00:01:04.900
consistent code, and reviewable decisions.
